WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.190
Return to Monday, the third of January,
twenty eleven, at eight.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.230
The recorded target is one
hundred and fifty four rentals.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.926
Zero weights and the completed training
window mean give a prediction of about one

00:00:14.926 --> 00:00:17.130
hundred and forty three point seven nine.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.060
This is a training calculation,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.240
rather than a claim that those fitted
statistics existed on January third.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:28.885
Prediction minus target gives
residual minus ten point two

00:00:28.885 --> 00:00:29.450
one.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.510
The forecast is too low.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.170
We now keep that same miss and
change how its loss values it.

00:00:38.452 --> 00:00:41.362
Half squared error has
slope equal to the residual,

00:00:41.852 --> 00:00:43.902
about minus ten point two one.

00:00:44.272 --> 00:00:47.462
Absolute error has slope minus one here.

00:00:48.032 --> 00:00:53.472
Huber with threshold ten uses a quadratic
region near zero and a linear region

00:00:53.472 --> 00:00:54.402
outside it.

00:00:54.732 --> 00:00:57.262
The threshold is measured
in rentals per hour.

00:00:57.752 --> 00:00:59.702
This residual is just outside,

00:01:00.172 --> 00:01:04.522
so its loss is ten times the
absolute residual minus five,

00:01:04.732 --> 00:01:06.782
about fifty two point zero six.

00:01:07.272 --> 00:01:09.842
Its slope is capped at minus ten.

00:01:10.272 --> 00:01:12.962
The signs agree because
the prediction is low;

00:01:13.432 --> 00:01:16.942
the magnitudes change how strongly
this row influences fitting.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:24.320
The implementation returns one loss
and one prediction slope for each row.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.680
It averages the loss values.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:31.590
Transposing the feature matrix and
multiplying by slopes gives the weight

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.120
contributions, divided by the row count.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.860
The intercept uses the slope mean.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:38.540
For our one row,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.700
the hour eight standardized value
is about four point seven eight,

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.420
so it multiplies each chosen slope.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:50.460
A full batch combines all eight thousand,
six hundred and forty five rows,

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.080
and their contributions
can oppose one another.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.680
At an exact match, our MAE branch
explicitly chooses subgradient zero.

00:02:00.942 --> 00:02:03.312
As an illustrative changed case,

00:02:03.482 --> 00:02:07.502
replace the target with four hundred
and fifty four while keeping the same

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:08.112
forecast.

00:02:08.622 --> 00:02:10.912
This is not a new recorded observation.

00:02:11.962 --> 00:02:15.912
The residual becomes about minus
three hundred and ten point two one.

00:02:16.522 --> 00:02:19.112
Squared error now pulls much harder,

00:02:19.362 --> 00:02:25.112
while absolute error still uses minus
one and Huber ten still uses minus ten.

00:02:25.682 --> 00:02:30.112
That is the mechanism behind lower
sensitivity to extreme residuals.

00:02:30.242 --> 00:02:33.012
It does not prove that a busy
commute should be ignored.

00:02:33.562 --> 00:02:37.632
A large real rental count may be
exactly the behavior we need to predict.

00:02:39.898 --> 00:02:44.498
Each objective receives the same three
rates and two hundred training only pilot

00:02:44.498 --> 00:02:45.328
updates.

00:02:45.878 --> 00:02:47.088
The calendar representation,

00:02:47.838 --> 00:02:49.008
scaler, initialization

00:02:49.718 --> 00:02:51.688
and Adam convention stay fixed.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:56.228
We choose a rate by the final
training objective within that loss.

00:02:56.898 --> 00:02:58.728
Every objective selects one here.

00:02:59.418 --> 00:03:02.808
The main fits reset state
and run two thousand updates.

00:03:03.658 --> 00:03:08.618
We never compare the numerical value
of MAE loss directly with Huber loss to

00:03:08.618 --> 00:03:09.408
choose a winner;

00:03:09.798 --> 00:03:10.768
their scales differ.

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:13.978
Fixed step Adam can oscillate,

00:03:13.978 --> 00:03:19.588
so the final update budget is explicit
and no exact nonsmooth optimum is claimed.

00:03:21.957 --> 00:03:27.010
Squared error gives later mean absolute
error about one hundred and sixteen point

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.027
seven three.

00:03:28.517 --> 00:03:31.887
MAE fitting gives one hundred
and seventeen point five six;

00:03:32.637 --> 00:03:35.427
the Huber variants are
also slightly worse here.

00:03:36.377 --> 00:03:39.307
MAE did lower training absolute error.

00:03:39.577 --> 00:03:42.367
That does not guarantee lower
error on another period.

00:03:42.997 --> 00:03:45.407
At the busy Monday target
of four hundred and twenty,

00:03:46.057 --> 00:03:50.467
MAE and Huber ten forecast around
two hundred and eighteen or nineteen,

00:03:50.937 --> 00:03:53.007
closer than squared error near
one hundred and seventy five.

00:03:54.297 --> 00:03:57.007
Other observations
offset that improvement.

00:03:57.717 --> 00:04:02.887
Keep the common validation metrics and raw
failures visible before preferring a loss.

